cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use DMAR, please cite it as below, together with the MBESS papers listed under references."
type: software
title: "DMAR: Design, Measurement, and Analysis in R"
authors:
  - family-names: Kelley
    given-names: Ken
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4756-8360"
    affiliation: "University of Notre Dame"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-09-21"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/yelleKneK/DMAR"
repository-artifact: "https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=DMAR"
url: "https://yelleknek.github.io/DMAR/"
abstract: >-
  Methods for design, measurement, and analysis, with the aim of being user
  friendly yet methodologically sound. DMAR emphasizes effect size estimation
  with confidence intervals; sample size planning through accuracy in parameter
  estimation (AIPE) and power analysis, including composite power; reliability,
  agreement, and measurement invariance; factor analysis and structural equation
  modeling; mediation analysis by model-based constrained optimization;
  equivalence and noninferiority testing; meta-analysis; and repeated measures,
  multivariate, ANOVA, and ANCOVA designs, with inference grounded in model
  comparison throughout. It is a more modern, more general, and greatly
  expanded reimagining of the MBESS package.
keywords:
  - R package
  - effect sizes
  - confidence intervals
  - sample size planning
  - accuracy in parameter estimation
  - power analysis
  - reliability
  - measurement invariance
  - structural equation modeling
  - mediation analysis
  - equivalence testing
  - meta-analysis
  - psychometrics
